        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 1473,
            "sid": 3,
            "position_x": 729,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 686,
            "height": 601,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "log1p gives the natural logarithm of 1 plus each value: log1p of x is log of (1 + x).\n\n0 comes out as 0, 1.718 as 1, -0.5 as -0.69. Because it starts from 1 + x, it is 0 at\n0 and well behaved near it, where log itself plunges to -infinity. Small values pass\nalmost unchanged (log1p of 0.01 is 0.00995); large ones are compressed like a log.\n\nUse it to compress something that is usually small but occasionally huge - a speed,\na count, a loudness, a jerk - when zero must stay zero. It is the reverse of exp then\n- 1.\n\nLists, NumPy arrays and PyTorch tensors go through element by element, keeping their\nshape. In the NumPy example at the bottom left, a random array (left) goes through\nlog1p cell by cell (right).\n\nSYNTAX:\nlog1p\n\nEXAMPLES:\nlog1p      compress a speed that is mostly small and sometimes huge\nlog1p      a log-like scale that keeps 0 at 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe data to be operated on. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe natural logarithm of 1 plus the input.\n\nBELOW -1:\n1 + x is then zero or negative, which has no logarithm. -1 gives -inf, and anything\nbelow it nan (not a number). Unlike log, log2 and log10, log1p does NOT take the\nabsolute value: inputs between -1 and 0 are valid and give negative results.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nlog     natural logarithm\nexp     e to the power of the input",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 678,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 585,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "log1p gives the natural logarithm of 1 plus each value: log1p of x is log of (1 + x).\n\n0 comes out as 0, 1.718 as 1, -0.5 as -0.69. Because it starts from 1 + x, it is 0 at\n0 and well behaved near it, where log itself plunges to -infinity. Small values pass\nalmost unchanged (log1p of 0.01 is 0.00995); large ones are compressed like a log.\n\nUse it to compress something that is usually small but occasionally huge - a speed,\na count, a loudness, a jerk - when zero must stay zero. It is the reverse of exp then\n- 1.\n\nLists, NumPy arrays and PyTorch tensors go through element by element, keeping their\nshape. In the NumPy example at the bottom left, a random array (left) goes through\nlog1p cell by cell (right).\n\nSYNTAX:\nlog1p\n\nEXAMPLES:\nlog1p      compress a speed that is mostly small and sometimes huge\nlog1p      a log-like scale that keeps 0 at 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe data to be operated on. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe natural logarithm of 1 plus the input.\n\nBELOW -1:\n1 + x is then zero or negative, which has no logarithm. -1 gives -inf, and anything\nbelow it nan (not a number). Unlike log, log2 and log10, log1p does NOT take the\nabsolute value: inputs between -1 and 0 are valid and give negative results.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nlog     natural logarithm\nexp     e to the power of the input"
        },
